WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.884
PayFlow, the made-up payments company, wants its checkout API to keep running when things break.

2
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.082
Things fail at every size.

3
00:00:11.082 --> 00:00:12.323
One server dies.

4
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.137
So run two copies.

5
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:17.011
But both copies sit in one data center.

6
00:00:17.011 --> 00:00:20.512
If it loses power or floods, both go down together.

7
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.128
So run copies in two or more availability zones.

8
00:00:24.128 --> 00:00:31.987
AWS describes a zone as one or more separate data centers, with their own power, networking and connectivity.

9
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:34.790
The zones are linked by fast private fiber.

10
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:40.104
Close enough for fast traffic between them, and far enough apart not to fail together.

11
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:46.039
The zones sit inside a region, a geographic area, such as eu-central-1 in Frankfurt.

12
00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:50.712
For most apps, two or three zones in one region is the right amount.

13
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:52.444
Now the network.

14
00:00:52.444 --> 00:00:57.767
A VPC is your own private network inside the provider.

15
00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:01.809
You give it a private block, 10.0.0.0/16.

16
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.381
Then you split it into subnets, smaller blocks.

17
00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:08.618
In AWS each subnet lives in exactly one zone.

18
00:01:08.618 --> 00:01:11.855
So an app in two zones needs subnets in both.

19
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.264
AWS keeps 5 addresses in every subnet.

20
00:01:16.264 --> 00:01:21.763
So a /24 gives you 251 usable addresses, not 256.

21
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:23.650
So which ones are public?

22
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.298
Not because of the name.

23
00:01:25.298 --> 00:01:26.945
The route table decides.

24
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.684
Every route table has a local route.

25
00:01:30.684 --> 00:01:37.984
10.0.0.0/16 goes to local, so all subnets in the VPC can reach each other.

26
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:49.681
A public subnet adds 0.0.0.0/0, the default route that means every address, to an internet gateway, the VPC's front door.

27
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.594
So the load balancer goes here, in both zones.

28
00:01:52.594 --> 00:01:56.152
It can have a public address, and the internet can reach it.

29
00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:01.975
A private subnet sends 0.0.0.0/0 to a NAT gateway instead.

30
00:02:01.975 --> 00:02:06.352
Each zone gets its own, in its public subnet.

31
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:09.655
The app and the database go in the private subnets.

32
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.720
Their machines can reach out, for example to download packages.

33
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.515
The NAT gateway sends them out, under its own public address.

34
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:21.117
But nobody outside can start a connection to them.

35
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:22.843
Only replies get back in.

36
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:31.441
So the request goes to the load balancer, and the load balancer forwards it to the app.

37
00:02:31.441 --> 00:02:34.255
That follows the local route.

38
00:02:34.255 --> 00:02:42.113
10.0.0.0/16 is more specific than 0.0.0.0/0, so the traffic stays inside the VPC.

39
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:45.996
Even a wrong firewall rule cannot expose the app directly.

40
00:02:45.996 --> 00:02:49.853
It has no public address, and no route in from the internet.

41
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.799
Now zone a loses power.

42
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:53.350
Zone b keeps working.

43
00:02:53.350 --> 00:03:02.123
It has its own load balancer, its own app, and its own NAT gateway, because each private subnet routes to the NAT in its own zone.

44
00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:07.841
One shared NAT gateway would be cheaper, and many teams accept that for test environments.

45
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.239
And watch the bill.

46
00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:14.520
The load balancer and the two NAT gateways bill every hour, even with no traffic.

47
00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.788
So that is the list you delete first, after a test.

48
00:03:17.933 --> 00:03:19.692
Spread copies across zones.

49
00:03:19.692 --> 00:03:24.773
And remember, the route table, not the name, makes a subnet public or private.
